In a recent thread I said that I like NAO's non-score based statistics,
especially the 'top all class players' and 'deepest dungeon reached with
ascension ratios'; because those are IMO more acceptable measures. I was
wrong, though, and I have to take back that valuation.

While stating "which requires good play on many/all character classes"
the 'top-all class players' list rather seems (without need) to just take
_highscore_ entries into account. I haven't noticed that before.

The effect was that my first ascended wizard class hasn't been taken
into account at all.[*] I thought an ascension and ascending a new role
would qualify for some points[**] according to the above statement; but
not even 2 cents.

Ascending 13 different roles with 3 million points each will not count!
Wow. I wasn't aware of that. And I guess you must be better than that.
Maybe, eg., after farming billions of points then stupidly dying before
you reach even Moloch's Sanctum? At least you'll appear in the tables,
then. ;-}

The consequence is, if you want to get your ascensions counted, you have
to delay your play by any means to populate your score points beyond the
current minimum highscore entry of 3+ million points. Boring. Pointless.
Uninteresting. (At least for me, since I don't go for score.)

I think I can ignore also that table in future... - never mind. :-}

Janis, dissipated

[*] And I had been sooo happy that the RNG had finally chosen a yet
unascended class for me. <sigh>

[**] I was thinking about the bonus of 800 for a new role and 400 for the
next two games of the same role plus the few bucks depending on the score.
IMO it would be a much more appropriate measure if every game would be
calculated according to the existing formula, and only the, say, top1000
entries of the overall _resulting values_ included in the table; instead
of not admitting an ascension in the first place. The present system has,
without need, also been biased on score.


